=========================================================
COMPOSITION OVER INHERITANCE
=========================================================

Definition
----------
Prefer Composition (HAS-A) over Inheritance (IS-A) whenever possible.

Composition means a class contains another object to reuse its behavior,
instead of inheriting from it.

=========================================================
WHY PREFER COMPOSITION?
=========================================================

Inheritance creates rigid class hierarchies.

Once a class inherits from another,
its behavior is fixed.

Composition is more flexible because behavior
can be changed by replacing composed objects.

=========================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE
(Payment + Discount)
=========================================================

Without Composition (Inheritance)
---------------------------------

Suppose we have:

Payment
|
|-- UPIPayment
|
|-- CardPayment


Business Requirement:

Support different discounts:

- No Discount
- Festival Discount
- Premium Discount


Using Inheritance:

UPIPayment
UPIPaymentWithFestivalDiscount
UPIPaymentWithPremiumDiscount

CardPayment
CardPaymentWithFestivalDiscount
CardPaymentWithPremiumDiscount


Every new discount creates more subclasses.

If there are:

5 Payment Types
5 Discount Types

Need 25 different classes.

Problems:

❌ Too many subclasses
❌ Difficult to maintain
❌ Difficult to extend


=========================================================
WITH COMPOSITION
=========================================================

Create a separate Discount abstraction.

Payment HAS-A Discount.

               Payment
                  |
                HAS-A
                  |
              Discount
              /   |    \
             /    |     \
    NoDiscount  Festival  Premium


Now Payment does NOT know how discount works.

It simply delegates the work to Discount.

Examples:

UPI + FestivalDiscount

UPI + PremiumDiscount

Card + NoDiscount

Card + FestivalDiscount


No new Payment subclasses are required.


=========================================================
WHY IS THIS BETTER?
=========================================================

Suppose tomorrow business asks for:

NewYearDiscount

Inheritance:

Need to create:

UPIPaymentWithNewYearDiscount
CardPaymentWithNewYearDiscount
...

Many new subclasses.


Composition:

Simply create:

class NewYearDiscount : public Discount
{
};

Done.

Existing Payment code does NOT change.


=========================================================
BENEFITS
=========================================================

✔ Loose coupling
✔ Better flexibility
✔ Easy to extend
✔ Better code reuse
✔ Easier testing
✔ Avoids subclass explosion
✔ Behavior can be changed without modifying existing classes

=========================================================
INTERVIEW ANSWER
=========================================================

"We prefer composition over inheritance because inheritance creates rigid 
  and tightly coupled class hierarchies. Composition provides a HAS-A relationship, 
    allowing behavior to be reused and changed by composing different objects 
    instead of creating many subclasses. This results in flexible, maintainable, and extensible software."

=========================================================
MEMORY TRICK
=========================================================

Inheritance

IS-A

Example:

Car IS-A Vehicle ✔


Composition

HAS-A

Examples:

Car HAS-A Engine ✔

Payment HAS-A Discount ✔

Notification HAS-A MessageService ✔


Rule:

If the relationship is "HAS-A", prefer Composition.
If the relationship is truly "IS-A", use Inheritance.
